    "['rule [parameter1] permit source [parameter2] [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set firewall filter 2001 term 10 from source-address 192.168.2.0/24', 'parent_command': ['set system login vty [parameter1] [parameter2]'], 'root': 'set system login vty [parameter1] [parameter2]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset firewall filter 2001 term 10 from source-address 192.168.2.0/24\\nset firewall filter 2001 term 10 then accept\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, ACLs are configured under `firewall filter` instead of `acl number`\\n2. The rule number becomes a `term` in Juniper's syntax\\n3. `permit` translates to `then accept` in Juniper\\n4. The source address and wildcard mask (192.168.2.0 0.0.0.255) converts to CIDR notation (192.168.2.0/24)\\n5. Both statements are required to complete the equivalent rule configuration\", 'trans_template': 'set firewall filter 2001 term 10 from source-address 192.168.2.0/24'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set firewall filter 2001 term 10 then accept', 'parent_command': ['set system login vty [parameter1] [parameter2]'], 'root': 'set system login vty [parameter1] [parameter2]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset firewall filter 2001 term 10 from source-address 192.168.2.0/24\\nset firewall filter 2001 term 10 then accept\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, ACLs are configured under `firewall filter` instead of `acl number`\\n2. The rule number becomes a `term` in Juniper's syntax\\n3. `permit` translates to `then accept` in Juniper\\n4. The source address and wildcard mask (192.168.2.0 0.0.0.255) converts to CIDR notation (192.168.2.0/24)\\n5. Both statements are required to complete the equivalent rule configuration\", 'trans_template': 'set firewall filter 2001 term 10 then accept'}]]": 1,
    "['route-policy [parameter1] permit node [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set policy-options policy-statement 1 term 1 from route-filter 192.168.0.0/24 exact', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set policy-options policy-statement 1 term 1 from route-filter 192.168.0.0/24 exact', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Juniper configuration command for the given HUAWEI `route-policy` statement:\\n\\n##\\nset policy-options policy-statement 1 term 1 from route-filter 192.168.0.0/24 exact\\nset policy-options policy-statement 1 term 1 then accept\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The HUAWEI `route-policy 1 permit node 1` matches ACL 2000 which permits 192.168.0.0/24\\n- In Juniper, we create a policy statement (equivalent to route-policy) with term 1 (equivalent to node 1)\\n- The `route-filter` matches the exact /24 prefix of 192.168.0.0 (equivalent to the ACL match)\\n- The `then accept` implements the `permit` action from the original configuration\", 'trans_template': 'set policy-options policy-statement 1 term 1 from route-filter 192.168.0.0/24 exact'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set policy-options policy-statement 1 term 1 then accept', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set policy-options policy-statement 1 term 1 then accept', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Juniper configuration command for the given HUAWEI `route-policy` statement:\\n\\n##\\nset policy-options policy-statement 1 term 1 from route-filter 192.168.0.0/24 exact\\nset policy-options policy-statement 1 term 1 then accept\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- The HUAWEI `route-policy 1 permit node 1` matches ACL 2000 which permits 192.168.0.0/24\\n- In Juniper, we create a policy statement (equivalent to route-policy) with term 1 (equivalent to node 1)\\n- The `route-filter` matches the exact /24 prefix of 192.168.0.0 (equivalent to the ACL match)\\n- The `then accept` implements the `permit` action from the original configuration\", 'trans_template